    "preview": "# Enterprise Data Governance Knowledge Guide for Toxicology Database\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis guide serves as a comprehensive semantic layer for the toxicology database, providing authoritative definitions, metric formulations, and governance standards. It is designed to ensure consistency, accuracy, and clarity in data usage across the enterprise.\n\n## 2. Core Entities & Fields\n\n### Business Entities\n- **Atom**: Represents the smallest unit of a molecule, characterized by its unique identifier, element type, and associated molecule.\n  - **atom_id**: Unique identifier for each atom.\n  - **molecule_id**: Identifier linking the atom to its parent molecule.\n  - **element**: Chemical element of the atom (e.g., hydrogen, carbon).\n\n- **Bond**: Represents the connection between atoms within a molecule, defined by its type and associated molecule.\n  - **bond_id**: Unique identifier for each bond.\n  - **molecule_id**: Identifier linking the bond to its parent molecule.\n  - **bond_type**: Type of bond (e.g., single '-', double '=', triple '#').\n\n- **Connected**: Represents the relationship between two atoms through a bond.\n  - **atom_id**: Identifier for the first atom in the bond.\n  - **atom_id2**: Identifier for the second atom in the bond.\n  - **bond_id**: Identifier for the bond connecting the two atoms.\n\n- **Molecule**: Represents a chemical compound, characterized by its unique identifier and carcinogenic label.\n  - **molecule_id**: Unique identifier for each molecule.\n  - **label**: Indicates whether the molecule is carcinogenic ('+') or non-carcinogenic ('-').\n\n## 3. Metric Definitions\n\n### Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)\n- **Most Common Bond Type**: Calculated as the bond type with the highest count across all bonds.\n  - Formula: `SELECT bond_type FROM (SELECT bond_type, COUNT(bond_id) FROM bond GROUP BY bond_type ORDER BY COUNT(bond_id) DESC LIMIT 1)`\n\n- **Average Number of Oxygen Atoms in Single-Bonded Molecules**: Average count of oxygen atoms in molecules with single bonds.\n  - Formula: `SELECT AVG(oxygen_count) FROM (SELECT molecule_id, COUNT(element) AS oxygen_count FROM atom INNER JOIN bond ON atom.molecule_id = bond.molecule_id WHERE bond.bond_type = '-' AND atom.element = 'o' GROUP BY atom.molecule_id)`\n\n- **Percentage of Carbon in Double-Bond Molecules**: Proportion of carbon atoms in molecules with double bonds.\n  - Formula: `SELECT CAST(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN element = 'c' THEN atom_id ELSE NULL END) AS REAL) * 100 / COUNT(DISTINCT atom_id) FROM atom INNER JOIN bond ON atom.molecule_id = bond.molecule_id WHERE bond.bond_type = '='`\n\n- **Carcinogenic Molecules with Triple Bonds**: Count of molecules labeled as carcinogenic with triple bonds.\n  - Formula: `SELECT DISTINCT molecule_id FROM bond INNER JOIN molecule ON bond.molecule_id = molecule.molecule_id WHERE bond.bond_type = '#' AND molecule.label = '+'`\n\n- **Least Numerous Element in Non-Carcinogenic Molecules**: Element with the lowest count in non-carcinogenic molecules.\n  - Formula: `SELECT element FROM (SELECT element, COUNT(DISTINCT molecule_id) FROM atom INNER JOIN molecule ON atom.molecule_id = molecule.molecule_id WHERE molecule.label = '-' GROUP BY element ORDER BY COUNT(DISTINCT molecule_id) ASC LIMIT 1)`\n\n## 4. Constraints & Conventions\n\n### Filtering Criteria\n- **Carcinogenic Label**: Use `label = '+'` for carcinogenic molecules and `label = '-'` for non-carcinogenic molecules.\n- **Bond Type**: Use `bond_type = '-'` for single bonds, `bond_type = '='` for double bonds, and `bond_type = '#'` for triple bonds.\n\n### Temporal Boundaries\n- **Fiscal Year Format**: Ensure all temporal data aligns with the fiscal year format YYYY-MM-DD.\n\n### Currency Formatting\n- **Currency**: Not applicable to this database.\n\n### Unit Conversion Rules\n- **Atomic Count**: Ensure all atomic counts are integer values.\n\n## 5. Exemplar Use Cases\n\n### Example 1: Most Common Bond Type\n- **Natural Language Explanation**: Determine the bond type that appears most frequently across ",
